Reason:
When `end` is ULONG_MAX, the computation `end - start + 1` wraps to 0, causing `len` to be 0 and the `if` condition to be false, skipping per-page invalidation. Additionally, the `for` loop adds `PAGE_SIZE` to `iova` without overflow detection; when `iova` wraps around, the condition `iova < end` becomes always true if `end` is ULONG_MAX, resulting in an infinite loop.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (end - start < RISCV_IOMMU_IOTLB_INVAL_LIMIT - 1) {
do {
    riscv_iommu_cmd_inval_set_addr(&cmd, iova);
    riscv_iommu_cmd_send(iommu, &cmd);
} while (!check_add_overflow(iova, PAGE_SIZE, &iova) && iova < end);
Reason:
The condition is rewritten as `end - start < RISCV_IOMMU_IOTLB_INVAL_LIMIT - 1` to avoid overflow by moving the `+1` to the right side. The `do-while` loop uses `check_add_overflow` to safely detect when `iova + PAGE_SIZE` would overflow, stopping the loop before an infinite wrap‑around occurs. This ensures correct per‑page invalidation even for the maximum `end` value.

